      "text": "To find the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle, we start by recalling a fundamental property of triangles: the sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's denote the measures of the three interior angles of the triangle as \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\). According to the property mentioned, we have:\n\\[ A + B + C = 180^\\circ. \\]\n\nThe mean value of the measures of the three angles is calculated by dividing the sum of the angles by the number of angles, which is 3. Therefore, the mean value is:\n\\[ \\text{Mean} = \\frac{A + B + C}{3} = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} = 60^\\circ. \\]\n\nThus, the mean value of the measures of the three interior angles of any triangle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
